# Tangling (§11)

This module is `lit tangle`. The prose is the specification. The chunks after it
are the implementation. Tangle extracts compilable source from `.nw` files. It is
the inverse of weave: it pulls a root chunk, and everything the root references,
out as a plain source file.

## Three sub-commands

`lit tangle <file.nw>…` writes every root chunk to the output directory (the
current directory by default). `lit tangle -R 'name'` writes a single chunk to
stdout. `lit tangle --check` tangles to memory, diffs the result against the disk,
and exits nonzero on any drift. The `--check` form exists for CI and for the
self-hosting flip (§13) — it is what proves a committed artifact still matches its
`.nw`.

The roots are the path-shaped chunk names the model already detects. The files
come from the CLI, the same as weave. A per-root line-pragma format is read from
`lit.toml` and defaults off. Writes go through a temp file and a rename, and lit
skips a write when the content is unchanged, so a build system that watches mtimes
does not rebuild for no reason. noweb ships `cpif` for exactly this reason.

## Why lit shells out to notangle

v1 shells out to noweb's `notangle`. This is correct by construction and carries
zero implementation risk. The cost is a runtime dependency on noweb, and no single
static binary.

noweb stays in the pipeline indefinitely. A Rust reimplementation of `markup` and
`notangle` is a contingency, not a scheduled milestone. lit does the work only
when the dependency actually costs something. Any one of these triggers justifies
it:

- Process-spawn latency dominates weave time on a real document. Measure first —
  caching may fix it.
- lit needs distribution to non-Nix users, where a static binary is the difference
  between adoption and not.
- A noweb version difference breaks the golden tests and upstream is unresponsive.
  noweb is stable and largely unmaintained, which cuts both ways.
- lit needs IR information `markup` does not expose, and the preprocessing around
  it becomes more code than a replacement would be.
- lit needs column-level tangle-to-chunk provenance for tangle-then-backout
  highlighting (§5.5). `notangle -L` records only which *line* a tangled line came
  from, not which *columns* are a chunk's own text against an expanded chunk-use
  hole. A lit-owned tangle can keep these breadcrumbs; noweb discards them. The
  payoff — always-correct, full-context highlighting — is reader-facing.

Until one of those bites, the dependency is a feature: it is a correct,
battle-tested implementation lit did not write, and it keeps `.nw` compatibility
honest by construction rather than by test.

